<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">Demerara</h1><p class="st-meta">1745 CE – 1812 CE · capital Stabroek</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Dutch plantation colony on the Demerara river, settled largely by British planters, whose capital Stabroek became Georgetown, Guyana's capital.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1745 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laurens-storm-van-s-gravesande">Laurens Storm van 's Gravesande</a>, commander of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/essequibo">Essequibo</a>, opened the Demerara river to settlement, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-west-india-company">Dutch West India Company</a> granted land to planters. Many settlers came from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/barbados">Barbados</a> and other British islands, attracted by fertile soil. Demerara soon outgrew Essequibo.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Demerara's plantations produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> worked by a large enslaved African population. A separate commander was appointed in 1750. The British seized the colony in 1781, and the French took it in 1782 and founded a new town at the river mouth, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> renamed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgetown-guyana">Stabroek</a> in 1784. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/court-of-policy">Court of Policy</a> of officials and planters governed with the commander.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> occupied Demerara again from 1796. In 1812 it was united with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/essequibo">Essequibo</a> as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/demerara-essequibo">Demerara-Essequibo</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgetown-guyana">Stabroek</a> was renamed Georgetown. The colony was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in 1814 and merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-guiana">British Guiana</a> in 1831, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> economy it dominated.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/demerara.jpg" alt="Demerara" width="320" height="159"><figcaption>Image: http://maps.bpl.org · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_Demerara,_1759.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1745 CE – 1812 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stabroek</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Laurens Storm van 's Gravesande</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Dutch Reformed Church, Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Dutch, English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/guyana">Guyana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Demerary</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1778&amp;focus=demerara" aria-label="Demerara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3070 1923 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Demerara lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3390 2179L5097 634M3390 2179L3380 2180M3390 2179L3390 2179"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5097 634h0M3380 2180h0M3390 2179h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3390 2179h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3390 2179h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1778&amp;focus=demerara">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
